## The question you're answering

A digital-marketing assistant working at a fashion retailer logs into
a colleague's email account, using a password the colleague had typed
in front of them several weeks earlier, in order to read about an
upcoming pay review the colleague is involved in deciding. The
assistant does not copy or share anything they read, and the email
content is left exactly as it was. Name the legislation the assistant
has breached, explain why the conduct is unlawful even
though no information leaves the account, and describe the impact for
the assistant and for the retailer. (4 marks)

The previous three worked examples taught you to write Impact (M.1.1),
to write Name (M.2.1), and to write Explain (M.3.1). This one teaches
something more specific. There are scenarios where students name an
Act cleanly, write a confident answer and lose every mark because the
Act they named is the wrong one for the conduct described.

This is the most common failure pattern on Paper 1 legislation
questions, and it is the pattern that costs the most marks per
mistake. A wrong-Act answer does not collapse to two marks out of
four. It collapses to zero, because the Explain and Impact paragraphs
end up describing the wrong rule applied to the wrong actor.

The diagnostic, which this worked example builds, is to read the
scenario for the *actor and what they did*, not for the *data*. The
presence of personal data inside a scenario does not automatically
mean the Data Protection Act is the lead Act. Many CMA, Equality and
IP scenarios contain personal data without DPA being primary.

As in the earlier worked examples, the answers below are highlighted
to show structure inline. The {% role name="name" %}Name{% /role %} is
tinted green, the {% role name="explain" %}Explain{% /role %} is
tinted gold, and the {% role name="impact" %}Impact{% /role %} is
tinted orange. A missing colour means the corresponding component is
absent or earns no mark.

## Answer One: confidently the wrong Act

> {% role name="name" %}The assistant has breached the Data Protection
> Act 2018 and the UK GDPR.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}The
> email account contains personal data about colleagues, including
> information about pay. Personal data must be processed lawfully,
> fairly and transparently under the first principle, and the
> assistant has accessed this data without any lawful basis. The data
> minimisation principle is also engaged because the assistant did not
> need this data for any work purpose.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}
> The Information Commissioner's Office could fine the retailer up to
> £17.5 million for failing to protect personal data, and the
> colleagues affected could lodge a complaint with the ICO.{% /role %}

This is the kind of answer markers see most often on this style of
question. Read at face value it looks competent. There is a Name, a
multi-sentence Explain that cites the actual principles, an Impact
that gets the fines roughly right. The problem is none of it is
answering the question the examiner asked.

**Name.** "The Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR." Confidently
named. Wrong Act. Zero marks. The question asks about the legislation
the *assistant* has breached. DPA principles bind the data controller,
which is the retailer, not the individual who reads an email they
were not authorised to access. The assistant has committed an offence
under the Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 1: unauthorised access to
computer material. The data inside the email is incidental to the
offence; the offence is complete the moment the assistant logs in,
whether the account contains pay review notes or shopping lists.

**Explain.** Citing the lawfulness and data minimisation principles is
internally correct. The principles do exist, they are described
accurately, and they are engaged by the underlying facts. But all of
this energy is being spent explaining rules that bind the wrong party
in this scenario. The assistant is not a data controller. The
assistant is an individual who has gained unauthorised access. Once
the Act is wrong, the Explain has nowhere correct to go. Zero marks.

**Impact.** The £17.5 million ICO ceiling is the right figure for the
wrong claim. ICO fines are issued to the controller (the retailer)
for breaches of UK GDPR; they are not issued to individuals for
reading an email. The colleagues lodging a complaint with the ICO is
also wrong-actor reasoning: their route, if they have one, is a
criminal complaint to the police under the CMA, plus an internal
grievance against the assistant. Zero marks.

Marks this would earn: 0 out of 4. A confident answer to a different
question.

The diagnostic worth taking from Answer One is the move that produced
the mistake. The student saw words like "email", "colleague",
"personal" and "pay" inside the scenario and reached for the Act
that protects personal data. The mental shortcut is "personal data therefore DPA". That
shortcut is wrong, and CA 4.1 contains several Acts that bite on
scenarios involving personal data without being primarily about that
data.

## Answer Two: hedged across both Acts

> {% role name="name" %}The assistant may have breached either the Data
> Protection Act 2018 or the Computer Misuse Act 1990, depending on
> how the scenario is interpreted.{% /role %} {% role name="explain" %}
> If the email account contains personal data about colleagues, the
> assistant has accessed personal data without a lawful basis, which
> engages the lawfulness principle under UK GDPR. If the act of
> logging in is treated as the main issue, the assistant has gained
> access to computer material without authorisation, which engages
> section 1 of the Computer Misuse Act.{% /role %} The assistant could
> face consequences under whichever Act the prosecution chooses to
> pursue.

A more cautious answer than Answer One. The student has seen that
both Acts are arguably present and has tried to play it safe by
naming both. Let's see how the marker reads this.

**Name.** Two Acts named, neither primarily. The marker is looking
for a confident identification of *the* Act the conduct breaches,
not an inventory of possibilities. Hedging on the Name suggests the
student has not made the diagnostic move the question is asking for:
distinguishing the actor's conduct from the surrounding context. In
practice, the marker will usually award the Name mark to one of the
two Acts named, provided one of them is the correct primary Act.
Here the marker would credit CMA. So: one mark earned, but only
because CMA happens to be on the list, not because the student
identified it as primary.

**Explain.** The same hedging that softens the Name softens the
Explain. Each half of the paragraph is correct *if* its branch of
the hedge is the right reading. But the student has not committed to
a reading, so the Explain is splitting one mark's worth of
explanation across two parallel paths instead of building one path
that earns a mark cleanly. The CMA branch (logging in is the offence)
is correct and would have earned the Explain mark on its own; bundling
it with the DPA branch dilutes the answer and leaves the marker
uncertain what the student actually thinks. Probably zero marks for
Explain, possibly one if the marker is generous.

**Impact.** Missing. The closing sentence ("consequences under
whichever Act the prosecution chooses") is not an Impact paragraph,
it is an evasion of the Impact question. No specific consequence is
named for the assistant. No specific consequence is named for the
retailer. Zero marks.

Marks this would earn: 1 out of 4. One for partial Name, zero for
diluted Explain, zero for missing Impact.

The diagnostic worth taking from Answer Two is that recognising both
Acts are present is the right insight, but stopping there is the
wrong move. Many Paper 1 scenarios engage two Acts. The work the
question is asking for is to name *which one is primary for the
conduct described* and treat the other (if relevant) as a secondary
exposure on a different actor.

## Answer Three: the full four marks

> {% role name="name" %}The assistant has committed an offence under
> the Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 1 (unauthorised access to
> computer material). The retailer may also have a separate exposure
> under the Data Protection Act 2018 as data controller.{% /role %}
> {% role name="explain" %}Section 1 of the CMA is engaged because the
> assistant has caused a computer to perform a function (logging in)
> with intent to secure access to data they were not authorised to
> access, and knew at the time that they were not authorised. The
> fact that the colleague typed the password in front of the assistant
> several weeks earlier does not amount to authorisation; CMA
> authorisation is task-specific, not credential-specific. The offence
> is complete at the point of access, with no need for any further
> action like copying or sharing. Separately, the retailer is the data
> controller for the personal data inside the email account, and may
> have breached the integrity and confidentiality principle under UK
> GDPR if its access controls allowed the colleague's password to be
> reused in this way.{% /role %} {% role name="impact" %}For the
> assistant, criminal liability under section 1 (up to two years'
> imprisonment, summary or indictable), almost certain dismissal for
> gross misconduct, and lasting reference damage. For the retailer,
> separate ICO scrutiny on the access-control failure, the cost of a
> forensic review of what the assistant accessed, and the obligation
> to rebuild password and access policies.{% /role %}

Walk through it.

**Name.** "Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 1." Primary Act named
cleanly, with the specific section identified. DPA is mentioned but
correctly framed as a *secondary exposure on a different actor*
(the retailer as data controller), not as an alternative primary Act
for the assistant. The marker reads this and sees a student who
understands the dual-Act structure of the scenario and has resolved
it correctly. Full Name mark earned.

**Explain.** The mechanism of the section 1 offence is spelled out
explicitly: a function caused on a computer, access secured to data
the actor knew they were not entitled to. The task-specific
authorisation principle is named
and applied to the specific fact that has tempted Answer One ("the
colleague typed the password in front of the assistant"). The
completeness rule (offence complete at point of access) is named, so
the marker knows the student has not been distracted by the "no
information leaves" sentence in the question stem. The retailer's
secondary DPA exposure is named in its right place. Two paragraphs of
real work that engage with the specific facts of the scenario rather
than defaulting to textbook definitions. Full Explain mark earned.

**Impact.** Concrete consequences, named separately for the two
actors. The assistant: criminal liability with the section 1 ceiling
named, employment consequences spelled out. The retailer: regulatory
exposure plus the operational cost. Both impacts are scenario-tied
(the password reuse, the access-control failure, the forensic review
of what was accessed during the unauthorised session, the
policy-rebuild work that follows). Full Impact mark
earned.

Marks this would earn: 4 out of 4.

## What to take from all three

The lesson here is one that costs students more marks than any other
single failure mode on Paper 1: reading the scenario for the *data*
rather than for the *actor*.

When a Paper 1 question gives you a scenario, before you write
anything, ask yourself two questions:

1. Who is the actor whose conduct the question is asking about?
2. What did the actor do?

Where the actor is an individual exceeding their access to a system,
the Act is almost always the Computer Misuse Act 1990. Where the
actor is an organisation handling personal data badly, the Act is
almost always the Data Protection Act 2018. Where the actor is an
employer making a hiring decision that disadvantages a protected
group, the Act is almost always the Equality Act 2010. Where the
actor is someone copying or distributing creative or technical work
without permission, the Act is almost always the Copyright, Designs
and Patents Act 1988.

The presence of personal data in the scenario tells you nothing on its
own. The presence of an email, a colleague, a pay review or a database
tells you nothing on its own. The question being asked is "what did
the actor do, and which Act makes that conduct unlawful". Anchor your
Name on the actor and the act, not on the surrounding furniture.

When a scenario engages two Acts (as the one above genuinely does),
the strongest answers name the primary Act for the conduct described
and note the secondary exposure on a different actor. Hedging across
two Acts as alternatives is a confession that the diagnostic move has
not been made.

Run this check on every Paper 1 legislation answer you write, before
you commit. If your Name and Explain are about the data, and the
question is about a person doing something, you have probably picked
the wrong Act.
